   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] Interpretation of shared spatial field",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi,</div><div><br></div><div><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">&quot;I am assuming that spatial dependence could also be in areas where there is low &quot;activity&quot; in terms of the absolute numbers of each species, but high spatial dependence?  That is, hot spots on the spatial field would not neccessarily correlate with areas where there is the highest number of either species. </span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span>&quot;</pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">If I understand hotspot correctly, it is where the spatial field takes large positive values. There you find large activity. If the activity is small, in a spatially dependent way, this will be picked up by the spatial field as large negative values.<br><br></span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">To interpret the spatial field it might be easier to think of it as a covariate, a beta*x. In some sense, the spatial field is just a fancy high-dimensional/nonparametric beta*x. You interpret the spatial field in much the same way as a shared covariate effect.<br></span></pre></div></div><div>Haakon</div><div><br></div><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Tue, 12 Feb 2019 at 10:00, &#39;Paul Mayo&#39; via R-inla discussion group &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">r-inla-disc...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">Hi\r\nAll,</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\"><br></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">We\r\nare using a joint model to determine the common spatial trends for two\r\ndifferent species based on work put forward in Sadykova et al. (2017)\r\n- </span><span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;color:rgb(102,17,204);border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\"><a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ece3.3081\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ece3.3081&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792442445135000&amp;usg=AOvVaw12kgpXBUnYxJcO15c3BCsU\"><span style=\"color:rgb(102,17,204)\">https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ece3.3081</span></a></span></span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">. In the paper, they use a shared spatial field for two\r\ndifferent species (using the copy function) in order to identify areas of high\r\noverlap between the species.  </span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\"><br></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">The full code for the joint spatial\r\nmodel is available from the authors here: </span><span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;color:rgb(102,17,204);border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"color:rgb(102,17,204)\"><a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/action/downloadSupplement?doi=10.1002%2Fece3.3081&amp;file=ece33081-sup-0002-AppendixS2.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/action/downloadSupplement?doi%3D10.1002%252Fece3.3081%26file%3Dece33081-sup-0002-AppendixS2.txt&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792442445135000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2UwY8WNtnTjXgmIhEZ2qk7\">https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/action/downloadSupplement?doi=10.1002%2Fece3.3081&amp;file=ece33081-sup-0002-AppendixS2.txt</a></span></span></span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;color:rgb(102,17,204);border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\"><br></span></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;color:rgb(102,17,204);border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\"><br></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">The\r\nspatial field portion of the model formula and INLA call are below designating\r\na shared spatial field (several covariates are also included) for 3 different\r\nspecies using the copy function (we are only using two species in our case):</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\"> </span><span lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:blue;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\"> </span><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\"> f(i.spat1, model=&quot;bym&quot;, graph = &quot;graph2.dat&quot;, hyper= param.spat01) +</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">  f(i.spat2, copy=&quot;i.spat1&quot;, fixed=F) +</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">  f(i.spat3, copy=&quot;i.spat1&quot;, fixed=F)</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\"> </span><span lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">  result.j1 &lt;- inla(formula,family=c(&quot;gamma&quot;,&quot;gamma&quot;,&quot;gamma&quot;), data=data, </span><span lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">               control.compute=list(dic=T), </span><span lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">               control.predictor = list(compute = TRUE), verbose=TRUE,</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">               control.fixed = list(expand.factor.strategy = &quot;inla&quot;)) </span><span lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">The shared spatial fields are then plotted (Figure 5 from the paper below):</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><img alt=\"image\" width=\"191\" height=\"400\" border=\"0\"></span><span lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">We have been able to plot similar figures for the spatial field (after including covariates as well) and have found similar hot spots. However, I was just hoping for some ideas on exactly how these spatial dependencies could be interpreted. </span><span lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">Sadykova et al. (2017) in section 4.2.3 of their paper describe hot spot areas as &quot;high activity areas of the coupled species&quot;. </span><span lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">I am assuming that spatial dependence could also be in areas where there is low &quot;activity&quot; in terms of the absolute numbers of each species, but high spatial dependence?  That is, hot spots on the spatial field would not neccessarily correlate with areas where there is the highest number of either species. </span><span lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></pre>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">In the case of a spatial field\r\nfor a single response variable, Zuur et al. 2017 (Spatial, Temporal and\r\nSpatial-Temporal Ecological<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">Data Analysis with R-INLA)\r\ndescribe an example of bird nets as follows:</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\" lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></pre>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">“A value of µ<sub>i</sub> = 0.8\r\nat a nest in one of these hotspots has an expected number of fledged chicks\r\nthat are around exp(0.8) = 2 larger than at a nest not in such a hotspot.” –\r\npage 318</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\" lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></pre>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">Is there a way to characterize\r\na shared spatial field in a similar manner? </span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\" lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\" lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0cm\" lang=\"EN-US\">Many thanks for any help!</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\" lang=\"EN-US\"><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></p></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;R-inla discussion group&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">r-inla-discussion...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nTo post to this group, send email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">r-inla-disc...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nVisit this group at <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/group/r-inla-discussion-group\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/group/r-inla-discussion-group&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792442445135000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0NM7QKeFC2cqpDWOjBtFby\">https://groups.google.com/group/r-inla-discussion-group</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792442445135000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3SbTN0IEiNud0LhElx2wTP\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.<br>\r\n</blockquote></div></div></div>\r\n"
